{"abstract":[{"text":"A bidirectional directed graph that generic code can mutate.","type":"text"}],"relationshipsSections":[{"title":"Inherits From","type":"inheritsFrom","kind":"relationships","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/BidirectionalDirectedGraph","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedGraph"]}],"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"],"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"protocol"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MutableDirectedGraph"},{"kind":"text","text":"<"},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedGraph\/Vertex","text":"Vertex","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols08DirectedA0P6VertexQa"},{"kind":"text","text":"> : "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/BidirectionalDirectedGraph","text":"BidirectionalDirectedGraph","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols021BidirectionalDirectedA0P"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"where"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Self"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedGraph\/Vertices-swift.associatedtype","text":"Vertices","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols08DirectedA0P8VerticesQa"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:Sl5IndexQa","text":"Index"},{"kind":"text","text":" == "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:Si","text":"Int"}]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Overview","anchor":"overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A wrapper that adds a rule, such as "},{"code":"DirectedAcyclicGraph","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", can own any mutable directed"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"representation as its base through this protocol and work on it without extra hashing."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Each vertex has a key: its index in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"type":"text","text":" ("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Vertices.Index == Int"},{"type":"text","text":"). On a dense"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"representation ("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"AdjacencyList"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DirectedPseudograph"},{"type":"text","text":") the key is the vertex’s slot, which is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"also its "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertexIndex(of:)"},{"type":"text","text":". On "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"StableDirectedGraph"},{"type":"text","text":" it is the vertex’s position, which never"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"changes while the vertex exists and may leave holes. The underscored members read and write the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"graph by key, so a wrapper hashes a vertex once and then works on keys."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"In addition to "},{"code":"BidirectionalDirectedGraph","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"’s laws:"}]},{"items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"_key(of: v)"},{"text":" is ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices.firstIndex(of: v)"},{"text":", and ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"_vertex(atKey:)"},{"text":" is its inverse.","type":"text"}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"For a vertex with key ","type":"text"},{"code":"k","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"_successors(ofKey: k)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"_predecessors(ofKey: k)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":",","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"_outEdges(ofKey: k)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"_inEdges(ofKey: k)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"_outDegree(ofKey: k)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"code":"_inDegree(ofKey: k)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"equal the unprefixed members for that vertex.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"_successorKeys(ofKey: k)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"code":"_predecessorKeys(ofKey: k)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" are the far ends’ keys, in the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"same order. ","type":"text"},{"code":"_sourceKey(ofEdgeAt:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"code":"_targetKey(ofEdgeAt:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" are the endpoints’ keys.","type":"text"}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"_insertVertex(_:)"},{"type":"text","text":" appends a vertex, which must not already be one, and returns its key,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"which is "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices.endIndex"},{"type":"text","text":" before the call. Keys of other vertices do not change."}]}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"_insertEdge(fromKey:toKey:)"},{"text":" inserts the edge and returns its position. On a simple graph","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"(","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"AdjacencyList"},{"text":"), an edge already present is not inserted again and its position is","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"returned. Inserting an edge changes no key.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"_removeVertex(atKey: k)"},{"type":"text","text":" removes the vertex and every edge at it. If the representation fills"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the hole by moving another vertex in (a swap-remove), it returns that vertex’s old key, and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"that vertex’s key becomes "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"k"},{"type":"text","text":". Otherwise it returns "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"nil"},{"type":"text","text":" and no other key changes."}]}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"_removeEdge(at:)"},{"type":"text","text":" removes the edge at a live position and returns it, and traps on any other"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"position. Removing an edge changes no key."}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"remove(v)"},{"type":"text","text":" is "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"_removeVertex(atKey: _key(of: v)!)"},{"type":"text","text":", returning the removed instance."}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"code":"_built(vertices:sourceKeys:targetKeys:reportingPositions:_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is ","type":"text"},{"code":"init()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", then","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"_insertVertex(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" for each vertex in order, then ","type":"text"},{"code":"_insertEdge(fromKey:toKey:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" for each pair","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"in order. It gives the same graph and the same position for each pair.","type":"text"}]}]}],"type":"unorderedList"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A wrapper may keep one table entry per 1024 keys, from the smallest live key to"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices.endIndex"},{"type":"text","text":", plus O(1) per live vertex. That matches the representation’s own page"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"table. On a dense representation the keys are "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0..<vertexCount"},{"type":"text","text":". On "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"StableDirectedGraph"},{"type":"text","text":" the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"span can exceed the vertex count, because removals leave holes and a decoded graph may start at"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"any position. Its decoder bounds the span at "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"16 × vertexCount + 64"},{"type":"text","text":" pages. Keys before the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"smallest live key cost nothing."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"_successorKeys(ofKey:)"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"_predecessorKeys(ofKey:)"},{"type":"text","text":" return the stored rows typed as"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"SuccessorIndices"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"PredecessorIndices"},{"type":"text","text":", whose elements here are keys. On a dense"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"representation keys and indices coincide. On "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"StableDirectedGraph"},{"type":"text","text":" the elements are positions,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"which are vertex indices only while the graph has no holes. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"successorIndices(ofIndex:)"},{"type":"text","text":" is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"then unavailable, but the key rows are not."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"code":"insert(edge:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is not a requirement because its return type varies by representation."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"AdjacencyList","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" returns "},{"code":"(inserted:memberAfterInsert:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", the multigraphs return the new"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"position, and a wrapper that rejects edges returns an optional. Generic code inserts an edge by"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"key with "},{"code":"_insertEdge(fromKey:toKey:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The underscored members are for wrappers inside the library. Callers use the representations’","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"own ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"insert"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"remove"},{"text":" members, which take vertices.","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"content"}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/mutabledirectedgraph"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/MutableDirectedGraph"},"topicSections":[{"title":"Initializers","generated":true,"anchor":"Initializers","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/MutableDirectedGraph\/init()"]},{"title":"Instance Methods","generated":true,"anchor":"Instance-Methods","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/MutableDirectedGraph\/insert(_:)","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/MutableDirectedGraph\/remove(_:)","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/MutableDirectedGraph\/remove(edge:)","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/MutableDirectedGraph\/removeAll(keepingCapacity:)","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/MutableDirectedGraph\/removeAllEdges(keepingCapacity:)","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/MutableDirectedGraph\/reserveCapacity(vertexCount:edgeCount:)"]}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols"]]},"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"major":0,"patch":0},"sections":[],"kind":"symbol","metadata":{"title":"MutableDirectedGraph","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"protocol","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MutableDirectedGraph","kind":"identifier"}